 * A cloak that provides half physical damage.  To keep it
   from being overpowered, it would be heavier than other
   cloaks (perhaps 200 or so) and would NOT provide any
   magic cancellation (so wearing it with DSM would make
   magic cancellation difficult to obtain; you could wear it
   with mithril, but then you're forgoing the DSM slot for
   reflection or a resistance, plus the weight).  It would be
   made of cloth and be fairly rare to encounter by normal
   mechanisms.  I'm not sure what the base cost should
   be, but I'm thinking somewhere around 500zm.

 * Greaves are armor that you wear on the shins (so,
   in NetHack terms, it would use the top half of the
   boot slot -- you could wear it together with low boots
   or iron shoes but not with most other boots).  Wearing
   only greaves or only low boots leaves you vulnerable
   to xan, but if you have both it's equivalent to high
   boots.  The advantage is that you can enchant both,
   so you can get an extra 4-5 AC bonus, but the cost
   is that you don't get the various bonus features the
   more popular models of boot offer (water walking
   or speed, mainly).  There could be elven greaves,
   for an extra couple of points of enchantability.

 * There should be rations that aren't vegetarian.
   Every military force I've ever heard of includes
   meat in some form in at least some of their diet,
   so it seems logical that soldiers would have a
   chance to be generated carrying meat rations.
   The meat would be preserved in some way
   (jerked, tinned like spam, pasteurized and
   shrink-wrapped, whatever), so they would keep
   the same as other rations and would not rot
   away like corpses.  They should have a higher
   nutrition-to-weight ratio than K-rats but take
   longer to eat.  I'm thinking maybe 500 nutrition,
   5 weight, and perhaps 3 turns to eat (same as
   cram).  (BTW, I have always found it odd that
   cram is vegetarian in NetHack.  Isn't tallow or
   lard or blubber a major ingredient in cram, along
   with grain flour?)

 * Also, rocks should be considerably more
   common laying around on the dungeon floor.
   It's basically a cave, after all.  Rocks should
   be virtually ubiquitous.  A player with a sling
   should not be constantly running out of them.
   (They are, after all, not a particularly desirable
   projectile.  They're pretty much what you use
   when you run out of better projectiles.  They
   shouldn't be hard to find.)


